{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"blowsy-actuarial-waxwing","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n\nAGENTS.md carries two pieces of guidance that read as contradictory when an agent is about to\nrun a gate:\n\n- **\"Bound every background process you spawn\"** says to bound helpers AT SPAWN in wall-clock\n  time (`timeout 120 …`), because an unbounded busy loop reparents to PID 1 and runs for days.\n- **\"Gate duration expectations\"** says `review-plan` takes 15-20 minutes and a\n  completion-verifier close 9-11 minutes.\n\nAn agent that reads the first rule and then backgrounds a `review-plan` will put a `timeout` on\nit. That is the wrong move and it is expensive: the gate is a billable multi-call LLM run, so\ntruncating it wastes the whole call, produces no verdict, and — worse — the truncation is easy\nto misread as a gate failure rather than a bound the agent imposed on itself.\n\nI nearly did exactly this in the session that filed this ticket, and the operator corrected it:\n\"Don't bound the background helper at spawn. 15-20 minutes is a guideline, not a hard limit.\"\n\n## Why the two rules do not actually conflict\n\nThe bounding rule targets an **unbounded** workload — `while True: pass`, `yes > /dev/null` —\nwhich has no reason to stop and no natural end. A gate run is a **bounded** workload that\nsimply takes a long time: it terminates on its own with a verdict. The section already says\n\"Prefer a bounded workload to an unbounded one. A loop that counts to a fixed bound and exits\ncannot leak; only an infinite one can.\" A gate is that bounded workload. The guidance is right;\nit just never says so where an agent about to run a gate will look.\n\n## Fix\n\nState the carve-out in the bounding section itself, and make the duration line say that the\nfigures are guidelines rather than ceilings.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] The bounding section explicitly excludes long-running gate/LLM operations from the\n      at-spawn `timeout` rule, and says why (bounded workload, terminates on its own).\n- [ ] The duration line states the figures are guidelines, not limits, and that a gate may\n      legitimately exceed them.\n- [ ] The carve-out names the concrete failure it prevents: a truncated gate wastes a billable\n      run and reads as a gate failure.\n\n## Verification\n\n    grep -n \"gate\" AGENTS.md   # the carve-out appears in the bounding section","id":"463a-e356-af2e-4f8b","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"AGENTS.md: the at-spawn bounding rule reads as applying to gate runs, which would truncate them"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787848833609521001,"uuid":"37c4982d-a8ac-433d-8112-5ece4db2a5c4"}